<!-- ACT 1: Mid-crisis opening + person in a moment + era bridge -->
    <h2 id="dawning">The Morning No One Could Explain</h2>

    <p>In 1981, David Ho was a young chief medical resident at Cedars-Sinai Medical Center in Los Angeles. One morning, a man was wheeled in gasping for air. Both lungs were failing. The diagnosis came back as Pneumocystis pneumonia, an infection that almost never appears in a healthy body. It shows up in cancer patients. In transplant patients. In people whose immune systems were already destroyed.</p>

    <p>Then they found a lesion in his brain. Toxoplasma, another infection that only strikes when the body has no defenses left. Then CMV in his gut. Three different diseases at once, all of them the kind that only grow when immunity is gone. Weeks later, another young man arrived with the same set of surprises. Then another. Within months, all of them were dying. Nobody could say why.</p>

    <p>You lived through 2020. Remember how it felt? A new virus. No vaccine. No treatment. Everyone counted the days. Now imagine that same fear, but with no hope of a vaccine on the horizon, and a doctor who can only tell you one thing: this will kill you. Not in old age. In a matter of months to a couple of years. That was AIDS.</p>

    <p>It did not stay in a few cities. By 1995, it was the leading cause of death for Americans aged 25 to 44. Pay attention to this number: in 1995, the worst year, roughly 50,000 Americans died of AIDS in a single year. The disease they first called "gay cancer" had reached every corner of the country.</p>

    <p>If you tested positive in 1995, you did not plan for retirement. You planned your funeral. That was not pessimism. That was the data. In the last forty-five years, AIDS has killed 44.2 million people, more than many wars have killed in all of history. This is the world David Ho walked into. And it is the world he refused to accept.</p>

<!-- ACT 2: The wrong turns -->
    <h2 id="azt">The Pill That Looked Like a Miracle</h2>

    <p>The first real hope arrived in 1987. A drug called AZT. It worked by jamming the virus's copying machine, the enzyme reverse transcriptase. In clinical trials, the results were so dramatic that the trial was stopped early, because it became unethical to keep giving people the placebo. The FDA approved it on a fast track in March 1987. People who could get it felt like they had won the lottery.</p>

    <p>However, there was a problem written into the virus itself. HIV is a copy error machine. Every time it replicates, it makes mistakes, and some of those mistakes let it slip past a single drug. Patients on AZT improved for a while. Then, months or a couple of years later, the virus broke through and they crashed. The hope curdled.</p>

    <h2 id="wall">Six Years Without a New Drug</h2>

    <p>Here is the number that explains the despair: from AZT's approval in 1987 until 1993, not a single new AIDS drug was approved. Zero. Six years. The best minds in medicine had one weapon, and it was failing in front of them.</p>

    <p>But the failures were not wasted. They taught Ho something everyone else had missed. He started measuring something nobody had measured properly before: the actual amount of virus circulating in the blood. What he found upended the textbook.</p>

<!-- ACT 3: The breakthrough -->
    <h2 id="turn">What David Ho Saw in the Blood</h2>

    <p>The textbook said HIV went quiet between infection and illness, a long "latency period" where the virus mostly slept. Ho's measurements said the opposite. I read this and had to stop. The virus was not sleeping. It was at war.</p>

    <p>He found that HIV produces billions of new virus particles every single day. It goes through an entire generation every one to two days. And because it copies sloppily, those billions of daily copies keep mutating. That is the whole trick of the virus. It does not beat you with strength. It beats you with numbers and mistakes. Give it one drug, and out of billions of daily copies, one will eventually mutate its way clear.</p>

    <p>Here is the part that matters. Ho's answer was not a stronger drug. It was a different shape of attack. If you hit the virus with several drugs at once, each one jamming a different stage of its life cycle, the virus faces a new problem. To escape, it would have to mutate against every drug at the same time. Ho said it plainly: "If you force the virus into a corner using multiple drugs, it is statistically improbable for it to become resistant to all of them at once."</p>

    <p>Do not hit it once. Hit it everywhere at once. That single reframe, combination therapy, is why you have never heard the word "monotherapy" attached to HIV today.</p>

    <h2 id="lazarus">The Patients Who Walked Out of Their Deathbeds</h2>

    <p>In 1995, the first drug in a brand new class, the protease inhibitors, reached patients. Suddenly doctors had two families of drugs attacking two different stages of the virus. Then came the real turning point at the biggest AIDS conference in the world, in Vancouver in 1996. Ho's team presented their results with combination therapy, what the world would call the "triple cocktail."</p>

    <p>The word that swept through that conference was not hopeful. It was astonished. Patients who had been bedridden, wasted, with almost no immune cells left, rebounded after two or three weeks of treatment. Some walked out of the hospital. Doctors started calling it the Lazarus effect, because people were getting up off their deathbeds. One of Ho's own patients, whom the clinic called Dodge, was patient number nine in that 1996 trial. He volunteered on purpose. His story is why the science has a human face.</p>

    <p>That same year, the number of new AIDS diagnoses in the United States fell for the first time since the epidemic began. AIDS stopped being the leading cause of death for Americans in their prime. Time magazine put Ho on its cover as Person of the Year in 1996, noting that some people make headlines while others make history.</p>

<!-- ACT 4: The result -->
    <h2 id="result">Then and Now, by the Numbers</h2>

    <p>The fix did not stop in one clinic. It had to reach the whole planet, and that took a second, harder war.</p>

    <p>Let us put the before and after side by side. Worldwide, AIDS deaths peaked in 2004 at 2.1 million people a year. In 2025: 570,000. That is a 73 percent drop. New infections peaked in 1994 at 3.5 million a year. In 2025: 1.2 million. At the end of 2025, 41 million people were living with HIV, and 32.1 million of them were taking antiretroviral therapy. More than three in four.</p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Two Data Points, Side by Side:</strong><br>
2004 peak: 2.1 million AIDS deaths in one year.<br>
2025: 570,000. A 73 percent drop. The virus that was a death sentence is now a daily pill.</p>

    <p>Here is the detail that changes how you think about it. A person on treatment with an undetectable viral load cannot pass the virus to their partner. The field calls it U=U, undetectable equals untransmittable. The treatment does not just save the patient. It disarms the virus. Treatment is prevention.</p>

    <p>However, the drugs were ruinously expensive in rich countries and nearly impossible to afford almost everywhere else. Ho remembered a woman from Africa at a conference asking quietly what a therapy in a rich country did for her, when people in her village could not afford an aspirin. The answer came in two parts. Generic manufacturers turned the therapy into a few pills a day for pennies. And in 2003, the United States launched PEPFAR. Over two decades it has saved 25 million lives, put more than 20 million people on treatment, and helped 5.5 million babies be born free of HIV.</p>

    <p>Here is the honest part. There is still no cure. Timothy Ray Brown, the "Berlin patient," became the first person ever cured of HIV in 2007 through a high-risk bone marrow transplant, and he spent the rest of his life pushing for a cure so more people would not be left behind. He died in 2020. His kind of cure cannot be scaled to millions. And in 2025, someone still died of an HIV-related illness every single minute. The science problem is mostly solved. The unfinished business is a justice problem: getting the medicine to the millions who still cannot reach it.</p>

<!-- ACT 5: The lesson -->
    <h2 id="lesson">The Pattern That Works When Everything Feels Impossible</h2>

    <p>What does this story teach us? Not that every disease gets cured. Not that medicine always wins. It teaches us the shape of a breakthrough. When one tool fails, the instinct is to reach for a stronger version of the same tool. HIV showed the opposite works: do not hit the problem once, hit it from three directions at the same time, and it becomes statistically impossible for it to escape.</p>

    <p>You have never had to think about HIV the way your parents' generation did. You can buy a test at a pharmacy. A friend can take a medicine that keeps them alive and makes them unable to pass the virus to anyone else. None of that was handed down. It was engineered by people who refused to accept that a disease with no vaccine and no cure had to be a death sentence.</p>

    <p>Here is the part that matters. Four decades ago, AIDS was the most hopeless problem in medicine. The people who fixed it did not know they would fix it. They kept counting the virus. They kept failing. And when the pattern of failure became clear, they changed the shape of the fight instead of giving up. The lesson is not "science saves us." It is this: when a problem feels unbeatable, do not look for a bigger hammer. Look for a second hammer, and a third. Then swing them all at once.</p>

